{"name":"Golem","hex":"#836e59","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"golem","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A humanoid creature made from clay, animated by magic. The most famous was made by Rabbi Loew in the 16th century to defend the Jews of Prague from a pogrom.","A humanoid creature made from any previously inanimate matter, such as wood or stone, animated by magic."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/golem"}],"entries":[{"title":"Golem","blocks":[{"text":"A golem is an animated anthropomorphic being in Jewish folklore that is created entirely from inanimate matter, usually clay or mud. The most famous golem narrative involves Rabbi Judah Loew ben Bezalel, the late-16th-century rabbi of Prague. According to Moment magazine, \"the golem is a highly mutable metaphor with seemingly limitless symbolism. It can be a victim or villain, man or woman—or sometimes both. Over the centuries, it has been used to connote war, community, isolation, hope, and despair.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Golem_%E2%80%93_Mikol%C3%A1%C5%A1_Ale%C5%A1_%28Star%C3%A9_pov%C4%9Bsti_%C4%8Desk%C3%A9%29.jpg/330px-Golem_%E2%80%93_Mikol%C3%A1%C5%A1_Ale%C5%A1_%28Star%C3%A9_pov%C4%9Bsti_%C4%8Desk%C3%A9%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golem","wikidata":"Q215085"},{"title":"Golem (Marvel Comics)","blocks":[{"text":"The Golem is the name of a number of fictional characters appearing in American comic books published by Marvel Comics. These include:"}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golem_(Marvel_Comics)","wikidata":"Q3546467"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Rabbi_L%C3%B6w_Saloun.JPG/500px-Rabbi_L%C3%B6w_Saloun.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Rabbi_L%C3%B6w_Saloun.JPG/1280px-Rabbi_L%C3%B6w_Saloun.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Rabbi_L%C3%B6w_Saloun.JPG/500px-Rabbi_L%C3%B6w_Saloun.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rabbi Loew statue at the New City Hall of Prague","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARabbi_L%C3%B6w_Saloun.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Old_New_Synagogue-back.jpg/500px-Old_New_Synagogue-back.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Old_New_Synagogue-back.jpg/1280px-Old_New_Synagogue-back.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Old_New_Synagogue-back.jpg/500px-Old_New_Synagogue-back.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Old New Synagogue of Prague with the rungs of the ladder to the attic on the wall. In the legend, the Golem was in the loft.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOld_New_Synagogue-back.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/%C3%9Ast%C4%9Bk_Jewish_museum.jpg/500px-%C3%9Ast%C4%9Bk_Jewish_museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/%C3%9Ast%C4%9Bk_Jewish_museum.jpg/1280px-%C3%9Ast%C4%9Bk_Jewish_museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/%C3%9Ast%C4%9Bk_Jewish_museum.jpg/500px-%C3%9Ast%C4%9Bk_Jewish_museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Úštěk Synagogue with a statue of a Golem in Úštěk","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%C3%9Ast%C4%9Bk_Jewish_museum.jpg"},{"srcset":"h